      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brookshire was named for Nathan Brookshire, a prominent landowner who helped shape the early community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.brookshiretx.gov/page/getting-to-know-brookshire",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the Missouri, Kansas and Texas Railroad turned Brookshire into a thriving agricultural hub in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/brookshire-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Brookshire’s fertile alluvial soil made it a prime spot for cotton, melons, corn, and pecans, boosting local trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/brookshire-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s long farming and ranching tradition dates back to its founding and still defines its rural character.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.brookshiretx.gov/page/getting-to-know-brookshire",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Brookshire hosts the Brookshire‑Pattison Library, part of the Waller County Library System and a community gathering spot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
